{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether a lottery is left-skewed — meaning a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (positive outcomes) compared to unfavorable ones. In this case, both lotteries have a positive outcome (2500 or 2400 Euros) and a zero outcome (no win). The favorable outcome is the monetary gain, and the unfavorable outcome is 0 Euros. The skewness is determined by comparing the probability of the favorable outcome to the unfavorable one. A higher probability of the favorable outcome (i.e., higher chance of winning) indicates greater left-skewness. \n\nLottery A: 34% chance of winning 2400 Euros, 66% chance of 0 Euros → 34% favorable → 66% unfavorable → favorable portion is 34%.\nLottery B: 33% chance of winning 2500 Euros, 67% chance of 0 Euros → 33% favorable → 67% unfavorable → favorable portion is 33%.\n\nTherefore, Lottery A has a higher probability of the favorable outcome (34% vs. 33%), meaning it is more left-skewed. According to the decision reason, the more left-skewed lottery (A) should be preferred. Thus, the decision reason is applicable and can be evaluated based on the probabilities of the favorable outcomes.",
  "step_2": "The individual mentions that the 1% difference in probabilities (33% vs. 34%) does not matter much to the decision. The individual states that the decision was made in favor of Lottery B because 'falls man gewinnt, hab ich mich deswegen für die Lotterie B entschieden für den höheren Gewinn' — meaning, 'if one wins, I chose Lottery B because of the higher prize.' This indicates that the primary justification for choosing Lottery B is the higher monetary gain (2500 Euros vs. 2400 Euros), not the skewness of the outcomes. The individual does not mention or reference any consideration of the distribution of probabilities or skewness. There is no reference to left-skewness, favorable probability weights, or any evaluation of how the probabilities are distributed between the win and loss outcomes. The deliberation process centers solely on the higher prize amount, not on the shape or skewness of the probability distribution.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual evaluates the lotteries based solely on the prize amount (higher prize in B) and dismisses the small difference in probabilities (1%) as irrelevant. The decision reason requires evaluating the relative size of the favorable probability (i.e., the chance of winning) to determine skewness. In this case, Lottery A has a higher favorable probability (34% vs. 33%), making it more left-skewed, and thus, according to the reason, should be preferred. However, the individual does not mention skewness or any analysis of probability distribution. Instead, the justification is based on the higher prize, not on the skewness. Therefore, the individual did not use the decision reason. The comparisons show a complete absence of reference to skewness or favorable probability weighting in the reported deliberation process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

