  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not considered by the individual. The individual does not discuss the distribution of probabilities across outcomes, nor does the individual compare the relative concentration of probability on the favorable side. Instead, the individual focuses on the absolute probabilities of winning and the prize amounts, concluding that B is more attractive due to its higher probability (25% vs. 20%). This reasoning is consistent with a focus on expected value or risk aversion, not skewness. The decision reason requires an assessment of whether one lottery has more probability mass on the favorable outcome (i.e., left-skewness), which is absent from the verbal report. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The comparison between the decision reason and the individual’s justification shows a complete mismatch in focus: the reason evaluates distributional shape, while the individual evaluates probability of winning and prize size. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
